WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:07.090
Even the best actors have their melodramatic
moments, but every once in awhile, a performance

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.710
goes from "a little over the top" to "insane."

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:16.230
Maybe these performances are so over the top
they circle back around to brilliant, or maybe

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.520
they're just embarrassing.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.110
Either way, these clips offer examples of
overacting at its finest.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.800
John Alderton in Zardoz

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.490
Everything about 1974's Zardoz is weird.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.500
It features Sean Connery with a long braid
and rocking the Borat swimsuit look for most

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.510
of the film.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:40.920
It's a bizarro, trippy movie about a godlike
creature called Zardoz who talks through a

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:47.239
giant stone head, a group of immortals who
don't believe in sex or sleep, and Connery

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:51.079
running around with his copious body hair
on display.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:54.149
But Connery doesn't earn the scene-chewing
crown.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:58.680
That honor goes to actor John Alderton, playing
an immortal named "Friend" ...

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.840
"I hate all women.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.600
Birth, fertility, superstition."

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.660
And that's just a taste.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.299
If this scene seems confusing, don't worry.

00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:14.479
It doesn't make any more sense in the context
of the film either.

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:18.240
"I will, I will not go to second level with
you!"

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.869
But you have applaud Alderton for making the
most of his odd moment in this terribly strange

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:24.990
film.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.890
Nicolas Cage in Vampire's Kiss

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:33.200
You can't talk about overacting without talking
about Nicolas Cage, and Vampire's Kiss is

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.560
one of his early moments of over-the-top glory.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.750
Long before his Wicker Man remake found him
screaming about bees and punching a woman

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:47.259
while wearing a bear suit, he released what
might actually be the craziest movie of his

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:48.259
career.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:53.310
1988's Vampire's Kiss stars Cage as an ad
exec who gets bitten and thinks he's turning

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:58.910
into a vampire—and apparently vampires are
100 percent insane, because that's how Cage

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.390
plays his metamorphosis …

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.670
"I never misfiled anything!

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:03.810
Not once!

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.520
Not one time!"

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.990
"I'm sure that you didn't."

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:11.890
Oh to be a fly on the wall as this scene was
filmed.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.120
If the crew wasn't desperately holding back
laughter, they must have been looking for

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.670
the exits.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.690
John Travolta in Battlefield Earth

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:25.480
John Travolta wanted to bring the central
mythology of Scientology to life with Battlefield

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.480
Earth.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.430
Instead, he created one of the worst movies
of all time—and gave a completely bizarre

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.170
performance in the bargain.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.569
Battlefield Earth is about a terrible race
of dreadlocked aliens who have enslaved humanity.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:42.590
Travolta stars as the main bad guy, Terl.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:48.280
Travolta decided Terl was a guy who loved
a good laugh, doling out his version of an

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.760
evil chuckle in almost every scene …

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.580
"I do what I can."

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.140
"It's a joke."

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.920
If the goal was to make these gross aliens
as unappealing as possible, then Travolta

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.360
actually did a perfect job.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.060
A job we never want to watch again.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.820
Michael Sheen in Twilight: Breaking Dawn - Part
2

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.850
Michael Sheen is a wonderful actor.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:26.920
He's turned in brilliant performances on TV
in Masters of Sex, and on the big screen in

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.190
Frost/Nixon, among many other notable roles.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:33.220
But when it came to Twilight: Breaking Dawn
– Part 2, he went a little crazy as the

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:34.330
evil Aro.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:39.600
In fact, in a series filled with unintentionally
funny moments, Sheen's bizarre giggle might

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.880
stand out as the most memorable ...

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.260
It's funny every time.

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:55.830
Sheen actually offered an explanation of his
big moment to MTV, saying that since his character

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:01.890
has been alive for centuries and centuries,
he's probably incredibly bored and gets hysterical

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.480
when something actually unexpected happens.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.310
It really does make perfect sense when you
think about it—which means Sheen managed

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:13.180
to turn an over-the-top moment into a secretly
brilliant acting choice.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:13.680
Bravo.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.520
Ellen McElduff in Maximum Overdrive

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:24.990
Author Stephen King decided he wanted to give
moviemaking a try, so he adapted his short

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.240
story "Trucks" into 1986's Maximum Overdrive.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.090
The good things about the film: a full score
by AC/DC.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.710
The bad things: everything else.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:39.240
A story about machines coming to life and
trying to kill all humans manages to make

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.250
even less sense than you'd think.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:45.710
This was King's first time directing a film,
and he later admitted that one major issue

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.740
with the movie was that his co-director was
cocaine.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.710
Despite all the problems, the film has one
incredible highlight: the waitress.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:57.280
Actress Ellen McElduff makes the most of her
limited screen time …

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.690
"We made you!

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.740
Didn't you understand?

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.460
You can't do this!"

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:07.919
If you're going to have a small part in a
crappy film, at least make it memorable.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:09.560
"You can't!

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.020
We made you!"

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.750
Brian Blessed in Flash Gordon

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:21.090
1980's space opera Flash Gordon sure had an
interesting cast: Brian Blessed, an acclaimed

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:27.110
British Shakespearean actor with a big voice
and personality, and Max von Sydow, a frequent

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:33.169
collaborator with Ingmar Bergman who's considered
one of Sweden's greatest actors, played second

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:37.530
fiddle to Sam J. Jones, a former contestant
on The Dating Game.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:41.460
Watching Flash Gordon, you get the sense that
Blessed knew he wasn't winning any Oscars

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.540
for the film, so he just had as much fun as
humanly possible playing a winged man who

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:51.590
flies around in leather briefs and a breastplate
while Queen plays on the soundtrack.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:52.820
Why not go over the top, right?

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:54.500
"Oh well.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.740
Who wants to live forever?

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:58.700
Ha ha ha!

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.280
Dive!"

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.539
Kenneth Branagh in Wild Wild West

00:06:05.539 --> 00:06:10.069
In yet another example of a Shakespearean
actor trying to bring a bit of theatrical

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:15.690
flair to a role in a very silly blockbuster,
Kenneth Branagh played the villainous Dr.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.650
Loveless in 1999's Wild Wild West.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:24.680
An infamous flop that snapped Will Smith's
remarkable '90s streak, the film is suspiciously

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:29.470
devoid of humor for an action thriller/sci-fi/comedy
hybrid.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:33.150
Among all the failed jokes, we have the spectacle
of Branagh.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:38.630
His portrayal of Loveless is so consistently
over the top, wildly veering from dramatic

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:42.960
to simply insane, he's at least always interesting
to watch.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.560
"It's me, dear friends!

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.259
Alive and kicking.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:51.660
Well, alive, anyway."

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.760
"Mi casa es su casa.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.340
Faites comme chez vous.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.560
Let the party begin!"

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:07.130
Branagh knows that a villain who winds up
in a giant steampunk spider is not a man for

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.130
subtlety.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.130
Thanks for watching!

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.380
